<h2>What can an AI image provide?</h2>
<p>An AI-generated image can be useful for establishing:</p>
<ul>
<li>Overall form and proportion</li>
<li>Visual character</li>
<li>Material and color direction</li>
<li>Important features</li>
<li>Intended environment or context</li>
<li>A starting point for discussion</li>
</ul>
<p>It is most effective as a reference—not as fabrication-ready information.</p>

<h2>What is usually missing?</h2>
<p>A single image may not define:</p>
<ul>
<li>Exact dimensions</li>
<li>Side and rear views</li>
<li>Wall or component thicknesses</li>
<li>Internal structure</li>
<li>Connections and assembly</li>
<li>Repeated elements</li>
<li>Material transitions</li>
<li>How the object meets its base or site</li>
</ul>
<p>AI images may also contain visual inconsistencies. Features can change between views, alignments may not work geometrically, and elements that appear convincing in an image may not be physically possible.</p>

<h2>What additional information is helpful?</h2>
<p>You do not need a complete drawing set to begin. Send whatever is available, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Approximate overall dimensions</li>
<li>Additional views or reference images</li>
<li>Sketches or marked-up images</li>
<li>Known materials and finishes</li>
<li>Important features that must be retained</li>
<li>The intended use of the physical model</li>
<li>Preferred model size or scale</li>
<li>Required completion date</li>
</ul>
<p>Even rough dimensions can provide a useful starting point.</p>

<h2>How does the process work?</h2>
<h3>1. Define the purpose</h3>
<p>We first establish who needs to understand the model and what it must help them evaluate, communicate, present, or approve.</p>
<h3>2. Review the reference material</h3>
<p>We identify which features are clear, which are ambiguous, and what additional information or client decisions are required.</p>
<h3>3. Develop the geometry</h3>
<p>When no usable 3D model exists, Digital Model Development is separately scoped to create the geometry and production information required for fabrication.</p>
<h3>4. Review and approve</h3>
<p>The developed geometry is reviewed with the client. Important dimensions, forms, components, and interpreted design decisions are approved before physical production begins.</p>
<h3>5. Prepare for fabrication</h3>
<p>The approved geometry is simplified, divided, and <a href="/model-making-process">prepared according to the selected materials, production methods, assembly, and finish requirements</a>.</p>

<h2>Who makes the design decisions?</h2>
<p>Carve can translate visual references into coordinated geometry, but the client remains involved in decisions that cannot be determined from the image alone.</p>
<p>The objective is not to guess what the AI intended. It is to establish a clear, reviewable physical interpretation before fabrication.</p>
